(() => {
 const entries={
 'frtb-map':[
 ['Imagine three questions asked of the same trade: what happens if the market moves a little, if an issuer defaults, and if a payoff feature behaves unexpectedly? A useful risk framework does not assume that answering the first also answers the other two.','Capital is a buffer against loss, not a prediction of the next loss. These layers translate different weaknesses into a common unit so they can be combined, while preserving the reason each charge exists.'],
 [['Could a delta-neutral trade still need capital?','Yes. It may retain volatility, curvature, default or residual risk. Neutralising one sensitivity does not neutralise the payoff.'],['Why not use one charge for everything?','Small continuous moves and discontinuous default losses behave differently. Separate charges make those distinctions explicit.'],['Would a larger RRAO always mean a riskier bank?','No. It reflects the eligible gross notional and prescribed category, not a full ranking of every risk the bank holds.'],['Is SA just a fallback for firms with poor models?','No. It is a prescribed capital method and a benchmark used even within the IMA framework.'],['Can a perfect market hedge leave default risk?','Yes, if the hedge fails at the same time as the issuer or references a different issuer, seniority or maturity.'],['Why distinguish capital from expected loss?','Capital concerns adverse outcomes and regulatory conservatism; expected loss averages good and bad outcomes.'],['Does adding a second charge necessarily double-count a risk?','Not necessarily. The charges target different effects, with specific rules to avoid inappropriate offsets and omissions.'],['Can an institution choose IMA simply because it is cheaper?','No. Permission, scope, data, validation and desk requirements govern its use.'],['What should you inspect when a capital number changes?','Trace whether exposure, sensitivities, weights, classifications, scenarios or eligibility changed. A total alone hides the cause.'],['What information is missing from these three opening sliders?','The underlying positions and the calculations producing each component. The following applets supply worked examples of those steps.']]],
 'sbm':[
 ['Consider two boats: if they move in the same direction, a strong common tide can amplify their combined movement. If their exposures oppose each other, that same common movement can create a hedge. Correlation is not automatically a benefit or a penalty.','The order of operations matters because the standardised method is a portfolio calculation. The three scenarios represent three consistent correlation environments for the entire portfolio, rather than a menu from which to choose a different environment for every line.'],
 [['Why can lower correlation increase a hedged portfolio’s charge?','The exposures offset less reliably when the factors move less closely together.'],['Why can higher correlation increase a directional portfolio’s charge?','Same-sign losses arrive together more strongly, reducing diversification.'],['Why net identical factors before weighting?','Those positions respond to the same defined shock. Keeping them artificially separate can misrepresent the actual net exposure.'],['Are two factors in one bucket identical?','No. Bucketing groups related risks; prescribed correlations still allow imperfect co-movement within the bucket.'],['Can an equity gain offset a GIRR charge in SBM?','The risk-class charges are added. There is no cross-class diversification in this aggregation.'],['Can negative vega cancel positive delta capital?','No. Delta and vega charges are calculated separately and added.'],['Should you add each class’s largest scenario charge?','No. Add all classes and risk types within each consistent scenario, then choose the largest portfolio total.'],['Why might the capped-Sb fallback be needed?','The prescribed cross-bucket expression can produce a negative radicand. The alternative definition restores a usable aggregation under the stated condition.'],['Does a negative curvature residual represent an extra loss?','No. It represents a gain beyond the delta approximation. The curvature aggregation restricts how pairs of such gains affect capital.'],['What would make a seemingly diversified result misleading?','Incorrect factor mapping, sign conventions, sensitivity units or exceptional-bucket treatment can create apparent benefits that the rules do not permit.']]],
 'rrao':[
 ['Picture two options with the same current delta. One pays smoothly; the other stops paying when a barrier is touched. Their local slopes can match while their behaviour after a larger move is very different.','Gross notional is deliberately a coarse measure. RRAO is a prescribed add-on for designated residual risks, not a fresh simulation of the precise probability that the unusual payoff feature will matter.'],
 [['Why can a near-zero delta coexist with RRAO?','A local slope does not capture path dependence, unusual underlyings or other qualifying residual risks.'],['Is every complicated derivative an exotic-underlying product?','No. Exotic underlying and other residual risk are different categories with different tests and rates.'],['Why does a weather-linked payoff raise a different question from a stock option?','Weather is outside the ordinary market-risk underlying set and may qualify as an exotic underlying.'],['Does central-clearing eligibility remove every RRAO charge?','No. The listed/clearing exclusion applies to other residual risks, not exotic-underlying risk.'],['Can a normal delta hedge reduce chargeable gross notional?','Not simply by reducing delta. Only the specified exclusions and qualifying matching arrangements apply.'],['What makes a matching offset different from an approximate hedge?','The prescribed matching conditions address the instrument’s relevant features, not just one sensitivity.'],['If matching notionals differ, is the whole trade exempt?','No. The unmatched residual notional remains relevant.'],['Should the same exotic notional also receive the other-risk rate here?','No. This example uses disjoint categories and applies the exotic rate to the exotic-underlying instrument.'],['Can a bank unilaterally replace an excessive-looking charge?','No. The instrument-specific alternative described in the source requires PRA permission.'],['What should a reviewer examine before accepting a zero RRAO?','The underlying, payoff features, gross notional and evidence for every claimed exclusion.']]],
 'drc':[
 ['Suppose a bond has already fallen from par to a distressed price. Charging the entire original principal as a fresh default loss would ignore that part of the loss is already in today’s valuation. Jump-to-default starts from what remains to be lost.','A hedge on the same borrower can directly reduce exposure only under the netting rules. A short on another borrower is a different proposition: the first may default while the second survives. The hedge-benefit ratio limits that second kind of relief.'],
 [['Why does current bond price affect JTD?','The relevant loss is the incremental fall from current value to default value. Already-recognised losses are not charged again as new JTD.'],['Why does seniority matter?','Different claims recover differently and may not provide a permitted same-obligor offset.'],['Would equal notionals in two issuers produce zero net JTD?','No. Different issuers do not directly net; default can occur in only one of them.'],['What does WtS limit?','It limits the benefit of weighted net short exposure after permitted direct netting.'],['Why does a very short maturity not scale exposure all the way to zero?','The SA maturity adjustment has a three-month floor.'],['Is the prescribed LGD a forecast of the next recovery?','No. It is a regulatory input for the relevant instrument category.'],['Can you apply the bond shortcut to every derivative?','No. Derivative default values depend on the contract and require the appropriate default revaluation.'],['Why are credit-spread and default charges both needed?','A spread widening is a continuous repricing risk; issuer default introduces a separate jump loss.'],['Can a pure net short bucket generate negative DRC capital?','No. The bucket charge has a zero floor.'],['Would a lower credit rating always change JTD itself?','Not necessarily. It changes the default risk weight; JTD is driven by valuation, default loss and permitted offsets.']]],
 'drc-sec':[
 ['Imagine a building whose lower floors absorb the first damage. Two tranches resemble claims on different floors: sharing the building does not mean they are damaged at the same point. Attachment, detachment and pool identity therefore matter.','The banking-book risk weight is an upstream input to this example. Separating it from the DRC aggregation helps you identify whether a capital change comes from tranche credit treatment or from the distribution of long and short default exposures.'],
 [['Why do equal attachment points not guarantee an offset?','The underlying pools can differ. Matching payoff boundaries alone does not satisfy the netting requirements.'],['What is different about ACTP’s hedge ratio?','It is computed across the whole ACTP rather than independently in each bucket.'],['Does a banking-book RW of 250% become a 250% DRC weight?','No. The tranche DRC weight is 8% of it, or 20%, before the applicable cash-position cap.'],['Why is the weight’s origin important?','A mistake in the securitisation hierarchy feeds directly into the DRC result even if aggregation is correct.'],['Could shifting a short between buckets change non-ACTP DRC?','Yes. The local hedge-benefit ratios and available within-bucket relief change.'],['Does non-ACTP allow diversification between its 45 buckets?','No. The bucket charges are added.'],['Why must an ACTP hedge not also sit in ordinary DRC?','That would misallocate the hedge and potentially count it twice across separate treatments.'],['Why can a tranche default model need valuation rather than a face-value shortcut?','The loss allocation and current value determine what remains at risk; face value alone does not describe the tranche.'],['Why does the ACTP negative-bucket term not activate in this app?','The supplied paragraph first floors each DRCb at zero. The subsequent negative-DRCb term is therefore zero under the literal implementation.'],['What remains to build before this becomes a trade-to-capital engine?','Instrument classification, JTD construction, maturity scaling, matching/replication checks and the banking-book securitisation hierarchy, among other controls.']]],
 'var-es':[
 ['A sign saying “dangerous water starts here” tells you where to be cautious, not how deep the water gets. VaR is the boundary sign. ES describes the average depth in the dangerous region.','The chosen horizon matters as much as confidence. A one-day 99% VaR used to assess daily forecasts is not interchangeable with a 10-day, liquidity-adjusted and stress-calibrated ES used in capital.'],
 [['Could two portfolios have equal VaR and different ES?','Yes. Their loss threshold can match while their outcomes farther into the tail differ greatly.'],['Why does adding loss only to the worst scenarios affect ES?','Those scenarios are part of the tail average, so their severity directly changes it.'],['Does 99% VaR mean losses cannot exceed the number?','No. Exceedances are possible by definition, and model error can also affect the forecast.'],['Why is confidence not enough to compare two risk numbers?','The horizon, positions, risk factors, calibration period and method must also match.'],['Is ES the worst possible loss?','No. It averages a tail of outcomes; even worse individual outcomes may exist.'],['Why include part of a boundary scenario?','The desired tail probability may not correspond to a whole number of equal-probability observations. Fractional weighting gives the intended mass.'],['Does a calm year guarantee a calm tail?','No. A sample can omit important stress events and structural changes. FRTB separately requires stress calibration.'],['Why retain VaR after moving market-risk capital to ES?','VaR supplies thresholds for the prescribed daily backtesting counts.'],['Can a smoother chart mean the estimate is more reliable?','Not necessarily. Smoothing can hide tail variation or data problems rather than improve prediction.'],['What would you ask before trusting a tail estimate?','Which scenarios, horizon, valuation assumptions, data and omitted risks generated the losses, and how was the estimate validated?']]],
 'liquidity':[
 ['Being able to quote a price is not the same as being able to unwind a large position at that price. A long liquidity horizon represents continued exposure while a risk is harder to exit or hedge.','The nested subsets are separate experiments on the same portfolio. Each asks what happens when only the slower factors move. A fast-moving hedge held fixed can change the result, so the subset numbers should come from revaluation rather than arbitrary percentages.'],
 [['Why can identical 10-day ES values lead to different adjusted ES?','Their exposures to slower risk factors may differ, giving different incremental horizon contributions.'],['Why not multiply everything by the longest horizon?','That would treat easily exited risks as if they were all equally illiquid and would ignore the prescribed nested-subset method.'],['What happens to factors outside a shocked subset?','Their values are held fixed in that particular partial ES calculation.'],['Must each narrower subset have lower ES?','No. Removing a hedge from the shocks can increase the measured risk of the remaining shocked factors.'],['Why does the 120-day layer have a weight of six?','It adds sixty days beyond the previous sixty-day horizon, divided by the ten-day base.'],['Are the displayed contribution bars added directly?','No. Their squares are summed and the square root is taken.'],['Can position maturity affect effective LH?','Yes. The article maps maturity to the nearest qualifying horizon and limits it by the factor’s subcategory horizon, with a ten-day minimum.'],['Does a liquid equity imply liquid equity volatility?','No. Spot and volatility can be assigned different liquidity horizons.'],['What should happen when a factor fits two categories?','Apply the prescribed mapping rules; ambiguity generally leads to the longer relevant horizon.'],['Why review factor mapping regularly?','Positions, markets and risk characteristics can change, making an old classification inappropriate.']]],
 'stress':[
 ['A reduced factor set is like a group of witnesses who were present both today and during the historical crisis. They connect the two periods, but the bank must show that these witnesses still describe a substantial share of today’s risk.','The expansion ratio and coverage test serve different purposes. The ratio scales the stressed result today; coverage tests whether the reduced set has remained representative across a recent window. Passing one does not mechanically imply passing the other.'],
 [['Why use a reduced set at all?','Some current factors lack reliable long stress history. A representative reduced set can provide a bridge while full-set current risk supplies the scaling.'],['Can a bank pick the calmest historical year?','No. It selects a continuous stress period to maximise the relevant stressed measure over the required search history.'],['Why is the FC/RC scaling floored at one?','Under this PRA formula it cannot scale the stressed reduced-set measure downward when RC exceeds FC.'],['Is today’s RC/FC ratio the 75% test?','No. The test averages sixty daily ratios.'],['Why is an average of ratios different from a ratio of averages?','Daily denominators vary; averaging each day’s relative coverage weights the observations differently.'],['Can reduced-set ES exceed full-set ES?','Yes. The full set may include hedging effects absent from the reduced set.'],['What does continued coverage failure imply?','After notification and the required update period, enough desks must leave IMA to restore the condition, with SA applied to them.'],['Should every class select its own unrelated stress year here?','The class-restricted measures use the stress period selected under the portfolio procedure described in the article.'],['Why reassess the chosen stress period?','The current portfolio can become most vulnerable to a different historical event.'],['What has to happen before these sliders become real ES inputs?','Calibrate scenarios, validate history and pricing, map factors and liquidity horizons, and calculate each partial ES on the correct set and period.']]],
 'constrained':[
 ['Suppose an equity book and an FX book appear to offset each other in recent history. That can be useful risk information, but capital should not rely entirely on a relationship that could weaken during a crisis.','The class sum and portfolio UES are two views of the same risk population. The former removes cross-class diversification; the latter can recognise it through the model. The fixed blend keeps both views in the result.'],
 [['What does “unconstrained” mean in this context?','The whole-portfolio UES can recognise modelled diversification across the five broad risk classes. It is still subject to other FRTB requirements.'],['Why also calculate each class alone?','The class-restricted measures reveal risk without cross-class diversification.'],['Can the 50% weight be chosen to lower capital?','No. It is prescribed in the supplied formula.'],['Does halving diversification mean halving total ES?','No. The blend recognises half the cross-class benefit relative to the class sum, not half the entire risk.'],['Are there seven IMA broad categories because SA has seven classes?','No. This IMA blend uses five broad categories; the three SA credit-spread classes do not become three separate IMA categories here.'],['Is the diversification slider itself a regulatory formula?','No. It constructs an illustrative portfolio UES. Real UES comes from a joint risk model and the required stress scaling.'],['What if there is no cross-class diversification?','If portfolio UES equals the class sum, the blend equals both.'],['Could a within-class hedge still help the class-restricted measure?','Yes. The restriction removes cross-class diversification, not all modelling of relationships within a class.'],['Why must class UES measures be stress-scaled separately?','Their full, reduced and stressed risk relationships can differ. One shared ratio need not represent each class.'],['What result would make you investigate the assumptions?','A large benefit driven by unstable correlations, data gaps or mismatched risk populations warrants review.']]],
 'rfet':[
 ['Think about learning a shop’s prices. A hundred price tags photographed on the same afternoon are not a hundred days of evidence. RFET counts distinct observation dates and requires the prices to represent the risk factor.','Evidence that a price could actually be traded is different from a convenient number in a database. That is why verifiability, arm’s-length conditions, size, mapping and auditability accompany the numerical tests.'],
 [['Do several trades on one date count as several dates?','No. At most one distinct observation date contributes to the count for the factor or bucket.'],['Can thirty observations still fail?','Yes, if a 90-day period contains fewer than four qualifying observations.'],['Can a hundred distinct eligible dates pass with a long gap?','The alternative 100-date route does not impose the same continuity condition. Other data requirements still apply.'],['Why reject indicative quotes?','They need not represent a bona fide competitive commitment to execute.'],['Could a bank trade solely to manufacture eligibility?','No. The source expressly prohibits transactions or quote commitments made solely to reach the count requirement.'],['Does a vendor label guarantee a valid price?','No. Evidence, representativeness, vendor processes and audit requirements must be met.'],['Can one price represent more than one risk factor?','Potentially, but only with conceptually sound mapping and the prescribed documentation, validation and notification.'],['Does passing RFET guarantee usable model history?','No. Data principles separately address risk capture, transformations and consistency.'],['Why can arbitrary surface bucketing be misleading?','It can share evidence between risks that do not actually have adequate observations. The prescribed or permitted bucket conditions matter.'],['What happens to risk when RFET fails?','It receives NMRF stress-scenario treatment rather than disappearing from capital.']]],
 'data':[
 ['A perfectly tidy price series can be less informative than a messy one. If its tidiness comes from removing difficult observations or smoothing jumps, a model can look stable precisely because the underlying risk has been removed from its inputs.','The four-source comparison is a reconciliation exercise about risk, not merely a demand that every price be identical. Different timestamps, methodologies and overlapping sources need to be understood in terms of their effect on ES.'],
 [['Why can a factor pass RFET but fail the data principles?','There may be enough verifiable dates while the series used to generate shocks still understates volatility, correlation or idiosyncratic risk.'],['Is smoother data always better data?','No. A transformation can suppress real risk.'],['Why compare model prices with valuation prices?','They should tell a coherent story about positions and market moves; material differences can understate risk or impair PLAT.'],['Why does overlapping vendor data matter?','Apparently independent sources may share the same observations, giving less corroboration than expected.'],['What can a market index proxy miss?','Name-specific and basis risk that the actual exposure retains.'],['Can a proxy be used differently in RTPL just to improve PLAT?','The rules require consistency or the specified basis-identification and capitalisation conditions.'],['Does a missing data point justify inserting any convenient value?','No. Backfilling and gap filling need clear, appropriate policies and validation.'],['Why re-estimate regression parameters regularly?','Relationships can change, making old coefficients misrepresent present risk.'],['What if a risk factor did not exist during the stress period?','A justified construction based on comparable stressed characteristics is needed; the absence of history does not create a free pass.'],['What should follow detected ES understatement?','Review and escalate it, then make justified adjustments or treat affected factors as non-modellable under the article.']]],
 'nmrf':[
 ['Imagine a rarely traded position with little evidence of how it moves. A quiet data series may reflect missing observations rather than a quiet market. SSRM asks what a severe shock could do despite that uncertainty.','The aggregation separates genuinely idiosyncratic risks from risks with shared drivers. Treating shared risks as independent would create a diversification benefit precisely where a common stress event could make them move together.'],
 [['Does “non-modellable” mean impossible to value?','No. It refers to eligibility for the modellable risk measure under the requirements, not a claim that no valuation model exists.'],['Why is there at least a twenty-day horizon?','The prescribed SSRM scaling has a minimum of twenty days even when the factor’s assigned LH is ten.'],['Could an NMRF have a small everyday sensitivity and a large stress charge?','Yes. Nonlinear behaviour and severe shocks can reveal losses that a small-move sensitivity misses.'],['Can a bank choose any convenient stress scenario?','No. It must meet the specified conservatism, history, methodology and validation requirements.'],['Why not put every NMRF in an idiosyncratic set?','Those sets require evidence of purely idiosyncratic risk and negligible relevant correlations.'],['What happens when a common driver affects several supposedly independent names?','The conditions for separate idiosyncratic treatment may fail; shared risk needs appropriate capture.'],['Is the 0.6 parameter a user-calibrated forecast?','No. It is the prescribed parameter in the displayed other-risk aggregation.'],['Does a proxy automatically solve the lack of data?','No. It needs justification and must produce a suitably conservative stress measure.'],['Is SSRM a substitute for all other capital charges?','No. It accompanies modellable ES, default capital and the applicable other requirements.'],['What if an acceptable extreme shock cannot be developed?','The source specifies a conservative regulatory fallback; it does not permit a zero charge.']]],
 'plat':[
 ['Imagine two thermometers. One always reads two degrees warmer, but both rise and fall in the same order. Their rank agreement can be excellent while their distributions differ. PLAT tests both aspects of the P&L story.','The comparison is about explaining frozen-position P&L using the risk framework. It is not a test of whether a trader made money, and it is not the same as asking how often losses exceeded VaR.'],
 [['Can perfect rank agreement coexist with a poor KS result?','Yes. A constant bias can preserve ordering while shifting the entire distribution.'],['Can similar distributions hide weak day-by-day agreement?','Yes. Two series can contain similar values on different days. Spearman helps detect that problem.'],['Why freeze positions for HPL and RTPL?','It isolates the effect of market changes and modelling differences from new trading activity.'],['Does profitable trading guarantee green PLAT?','No. PLAT concerns agreement between two modelled P&L series, not trading profitability.'],['Why does the previous quarter matter in the intermediate zone?','It distinguishes orange desks previously on SA from yellow desks under the article’s transition logic.'],['Is a score exactly at 0.8 automatically green?','No. The green rank criterion is strictly greater than 0.8, and the KS criterion is also strict.'],['Can one red metric be rescued by the other being green?','No. Either red criterion triggers the red zone.'],['Why include NMRF effects in the risk-theoretical story?','RTPL reflects risk factors in the risk model whose shocks support either ES or SSRM, under the stated rules.'],['May data alignment erase any unwanted difference?','No. Alignment is permitted only within specified conditions; it cannot conceal missing risk capture.'],['Why does the app distinguish 2028 from steady state?','The supplied instrument deems permitted desks green for capital during that transition year, which changes the consequences of measured scores.']]],
 'backtest':[
 ['A weather forecast that promises a rare event will sometimes be surprised. The useful question is whether surprises occur too frequently or expose a systematic blind spot. Backtesting applies that idea to predicted loss thresholds.','Desk and portfolio tests ask different operational questions. One decides whether a desk’s modelling can remain eligible; the other raises conservatism for the permitted portfolio. Their scopes and thresholds should not be mixed.'],
 [['Does one VaR breach prove that the model is wrong?','No. Breaches are expected occasionally. Their frequency, causes and pattern need assessment.'],['Why test both actual and hypothetical P&L?','They expose different issues: realised trading activity and valuation changes versus the behaviour of frozen positions.'],['Does passing the 99% desk test guarantee passing at 97.5%?','No. There can be many additional losses beyond the lower threshold.'],['Why can the 97.5% breach count not be below the 99% count here?','For consistent nested loss thresholds, every loss beyond the higher 99% threshold is also beyond the lower threshold.'],['Does the thirteenth 99% desk breach equal the twelfth?','No. Twelve is within the stated limit; thirteen exceeds it.'],['Can a larger firm multiplier cure a failed desk?','No. Portfolio multiplier conservatism and desk eligibility are separate rules.'],['Why use the greater portfolio HPL/APL count?','The rule avoids choosing the more favourable of the two backtesting histories.'],['Should a missing VaR day be silently omitted?','No. The source counts missing valuation or VaR assessment as an overshooting in the stated daily rule.'],['Can an analyst discard an exception attributed to an NMRF?','Not unilaterally. Exclusion requires the specified conditions and PRA permission.'],['Why investigate breaches even within the permitted count?','They can reveal emerging risk deficiencies before a formal threshold is crossed.']]],
 'ima-drc':[
 ['Twenty borrowers can have identical individual default probabilities yet very different collective outcomes. If each fails for unrelated reasons, losses spread out; if they all depend on the same downturn, severe clusters become more plausible.','A discrete portfolio also produces a stepped tail measure. Moving a model input slightly may leave the selected default count unchanged, then move it by a whole issuer. That is a feature to understand, not smooth away without explanation.'],
 [['Can expected loss stay fixed while tail risk increases?','Yes. Default dependence changes joint outcomes without necessarily changing each issuer’s marginal PD.'],['Why is default capital separate from ES/SSRM?','It targets issuer-default losses under its own horizon and confidence requirements, incremental to the other risks.'],['Why not infer PD solely from today’s spread?','Spreads include other effects and may be unstable. The source requires historical default-related evidence and restricts purely market-implied PDs.'],['What does the PD floor prevent?','It prevents a model from assigning a lower-than-prescribed default probability to an exposure.'],['Is fixed LGD sufficient for a full internal model?','No. Recovery dependence, seniority and economic-cycle effects must be captured appropriately.'],['Why can a hedge fail just before default?','Its maturity, basis, seniority or maintainability may differ from the hedged position as the credit event approaches.'],['Why is the toy one-factor model not a compliant engine?','The source requires additional systematic-factor structure, empirical calibration, recovery effects, nonlinear risk and other controls that the illustration omits.'],['Does the latest weekly measure always determine capital?','No. The default component takes the greater of the latest measure and the preceding twelve-week average.'],['Why can the quantile move in jumps?','With equal discrete exposures, the threshold corresponds to a whole number of defaults.'],['What validation would a concentrated portfolio need?','Tests of joint defaults, concentration, parameter uncertainty, hedges and tail stability, not merely agreement with average historical loss.']]],
 'capital':[
 ['Capital assembly has both measurement and governance inputs. A change can come from market risk, recent average risk, default risk, a model-quality surcharge or a desk moving to SA. Keeping those steps visible explains the final number.','The all-desk SA benchmark is not an unconditional lid on capital in this PRA formula. A separate nonnegative term preserves an excess when eligible IMA capital is higher than the corresponding SA benchmark.'],
 [['Why compare yesterday’s risk with recent averages?','A single quiet day should not immediately remove capital supported by a sustained recent risk level.'],['Is mc applied to SSRM as well as average ES?','Not in the displayed formula. It multiplies average ES, after which average SSRM is added.'],['Can you maximise ES and SSRM separately before adding them?','That is not the prescribed branch comparison. Compare the two stated sums.'],['Why does DRC use a different averaging window?','Its rule specifies the latest measure versus the preceding twelve-week average, separately from the sixty-business-day ES/SSRM calculation.'],['What does a yellow desk contribute beyond model risk?','It can generate a surcharge based on its share of summed desk SA and the gap between the combined SA and IMA measures.'],['Is yellow share the same as yellow SA divided by combined-portfolio SA?','No. Its denominator sums individual green/yellow desk SA measures, which can differ from SA of the combined portfolio.'],['What belongs in CU?','The specified SA fallback portfolio, including non-permitted and orange/red or failing desks.'],['Is SAall always a hard ceiling on total capital?','No. The additional max(IMA − SAg&y, 0) term can raise total above it.'],['Why calculate the SA benchmarks at their exact scopes?','Diversification and aggregation depend on the portfolio; adding unrelated desk numbers may not produce the required combined measure.'],['What other capital can sit beyond this walkthrough?','Institution-specific risk-capture additions and separate capital frameworks may apply; this is not the whole-bank capital calculation.']]],
 'governance':[
 ['A reliable speedometer is useful only if it belongs to the vehicle being driven and is checked when the vehicle changes. Similarly, an approved risk model has a defined scope, data population, process and set of responsible people.','A passing statistical test is evidence, not a substitute for judgement and controls. A model can pass recent tests while missing a new product’s basis risk or depending on an unstable proxy. Validation has to challenge those possibilities directly.'],
 [['Can a position be moved between books solely to obtain a lower charge?','The trading-book boundary and transfer restrictions prevent treating classification as a free capital optimisation choice.'],['Does desk permission cover every possible product?','No. Scope and ineligible-position rules still apply.'],['Can an ineligible securitisation simply inherit a desk’s IMA status?','No. Ineligible positions are treated separately under the specified SA approach.'],['Does passing PLAT establish full model validity?','No. Independent validation also examines assumptions, basis risk, concentrations, proxies and other material risks.'],['Why does a model change need governance?','It can alter risk measurement, scope or implementation integrity even if the user interface looks the same.'],['Who should challenge the trading unit’s model results?','Independent risk control and validation, supported by senior management oversight and independent review.'],['Why perform reverse stress tests?','They ask what conditions could break the portfolio or model, rather than only measuring losses in selected familiar scenarios.'],['Are the 2027 legacy model rules the same as FRTB ES?','No. The transitional annex contains a separate VaR/stressed-VaR framework and relevant additional charges.'],['Does deemed-green status in 2028 waive every requirement?','No. It changes the stated PLAT capital treatment and specified application standard, not every permission, data or risk-management obligation.'],['What should a learner check before applying any applet to a real institution?','The applicable date, jurisdiction, permission, portfolio scope, source text and validated operational implementation.']]]
 };
 const specific={
 girr:[['Could a five-year hedge remove all yield-curve risk?','No. Other tenors and curves can move differently, and inflation or basis factors may remain.'],['Why do GBP and EUR occupy different buckets?','Currency defines GIRR buckets; their risk-free curves are not the same factor.'],['Why is the curvature shock larger than this lab’s five-year delta RW?','The whole curve uses its highest prescribed delta weight for the curvature parallel shift.']],
 csr:[['Could a bank bond and a retail bond be treated as one issuer?','No. They are different names and can respond differently even when both are investment grade.'],['Why keep bond/CDS basis visible?','A bond and a CDS spread can diverge, so their offset is not represented as perfect identity across all factor definitions.'],['Does a narrow credit spread eliminate default capital?','No. Spread sensitivity and the incremental loss at default are different risks.']],
 nonactp:[['Why focus on tranche spread rather than the loans one by one?','That is the prescribed non-ACTP factor mapping, reflecting the tranche exposure being valued.'],['Do two different pools become identical because their tranches have the same rating?','No. Ratings do not establish the same tranche or pool and do not remove basis risk.'],['Why is bucket 25 handled separately?','The other bucket receives no recognised diversification or hedging benefit under the specified treatment.']],
 actp:[['Why does ACTP look through to underlying issuers?','Correlation-trading risk depends on the underlying issuer spread structure rather than only the quoted tranche spread.'],['Can an ACTP hedge also be allocated to ordinary DRC?','It must follow the ACTP allocation rules to avoid inappropriate double use.'],['Are ACTP weights the same as ordinary CSR weights?','No. ACTP uses its own prescribed weights despite related sector bucketing.']],
 equity:[['Can an index hedge remove all company-specific risk?','No. Individual stocks retain name-specific moves relative to the index.'],['Why does repo receive different treatment from equity spot?','Repo is a rate risk with its own delta weights and does not receive equity vega or curvature charges.'],['Is the large-cap boundary simply a familiar market label?','No. The supplied PRA instrument defines it using a market capitalisation threshold above £1.60 billion.']],
 commodity:[['Why can Brent and WTI fail to offset exactly?','Their supply, location and delivery characteristics differ despite a shared oil-market driver.'],['Why might two electricity contracts represent different commodities?','Delivery region and period can create materially different risk factors under the prescribed mapping.'],['What changes when moving from commodity delta to curvature mapping?','Curvature groups the commodity’s maturity curve and does not distinguish delivery location.']],
 fx:[['Why does reporting currency change the factor map?','Delta and curvature measure rates against the reporting or permitted base currency, so the reference currency determines the exposures.'],['Should two USD/GBP delta sensitivities remain two independent factors?','No. There is a single net sensitivity in that pair’s delta bucket; net them first.'],['Can every FX curvature exposure be divided by 1.5?','No. The divisor is conditional, and the broader treatment requires the specified PRA permission.']]
 };
 window.LEARNING_CONTENT={};
 FRTB_LESSONS.forEach(l=>{const entry=entries[l.id];if(entry){window.LEARNING_CONTENT[l.id]={intuition:entry[0],questions:entry[1]};return;}const c=FRTB_CLASSES.find(c=>c.id===l.id);if(!c)throw Error('Missing learning content '+l.id);window.LEARNING_CONTENT[l.id]={intuition:[`For ${c.name}, start by asking what must move for the position to lose value. ${c.intuition} A net amount is meaningful only after identifying whether the positions truly share the same risk factor.`,`Explore the three components as three different experiments. Delta asks about a small underlying move; vega changes implied volatility; curvature reprices under a large prescribed shock and removes the linear delta estimate. A hedge that works well in one experiment need not work well in the others.`],questions:[...specific[l.id],[`In this ${c.name} example, why can opposing inputs still leave a positive charge?`,'The inputs can belong to different risk factors or buckets. Prescribed correlations recognise only partial hedging between them.'],[`What does the ${c.name} vega view multiply by current volatility?`,'The unscaled sensitivity dV/dσ. Applying the vega risk weight to raw dV/dσ without this volatility scaling would use the wrong regulatory input.'],[`Why does ${c.name} curvature remove the delta contribution?`,'It isolates the nonlinear remainder of the full shock repricing, rather than charging the linear effect again as curvature.'],[`Can a negative ${c.name} CVR be read as an additional loss?`,'No. It is a gain relative to the delta estimate. The aggregation specifically limits how pairs of negative CVRs enter correlation terms.'],[`Would the largest scenario in this ${c.name} view always determine the whole-portfolio SBM?`,'No. The portfolio selects the largest total after all risk classes and components are added within each scenario.'],[`Why are the ${c.name} factor labels as important as the entered numbers?`,'They determine netting, buckets, weights and correlations. A numerically correct formula applied to the wrong factor map is still wrong.'],[`What must be replaced before this ${c.name} lab can process actual trades?`,'The synthetic inputs must come from validated instrument repricing, correct factor mapping and all applicable netting and special-bucket rules. The teaching portfolio is not a universal reporting engine.']]};});
})();
